Beryllium: fingerprint noise draws depend on the whole per-site token

Purpose: Helium's noise (helium/core/noise/core.patch) gives every site a
  64-bit token per session and derives the noise from a running hash of
  the token, the pixel's coordinates and its content. The hash step is
  FNV-1a's (xor a value in, multiply by the FNV prime), and
  HeliumNoiseHash::GetValueBelow() hands out the hash's bits from the low
  end. In a multiplication the low k bits of the product depend only on
  the low k bits of the factors, so the first draws after an Update() saw
  only the lowest bits of the token:
  - canvas, smooth areas: whether a pixel moves is GetValueBelow(4) on
    the coordinate hash, which is its low two bits. Which pixels of a
    flat fill move was therefore one of four sets, chosen by two bits of
    the token, and the four sets are the same in every browser that
    carries this code;
  - the direction of the change is GetValueBelow(2) after a two-bit draw,
    which is bit 2: three bits of the token.
  Only draws whose bound is not a power of two (the channel, below 3)
  depended on more. A flat fill had 24 possible readbacks in all, and two
  sites in one session moved exactly the same pixels with probability one
  in four.
  Found by the owner's suite runs: the per-site test compares which pixels
  two origins move, and in three of the eight runs that made the comparison
  (one of four on 0.20.3.14, none of two on 0.20.3.15, both on 0.20.3.16)
  both origins moved the same pixels. A model of the hash in Python gives
  1031, 998, 1085 and 982 pixels for token low bits 0 to 3. Every pixel
  count in those reports is one of the four; the 982 pixels listed in the
  first 0.20.3.16 report are exactly the model's set for low bits 3, and
  the 1031 listed in the second, a count no report had shown until the
  model gave it, are exactly its set for low bits 0. Helium's file compiled
  outside the tree against stub headers gives the same four sets.
  This patch takes the bits from a finalized copy of the running hash (the
  SplitMix64 finalizer, already used by Helium's canvas code for the
  coordinates): every bit handed out depends on every bit of the hash and
  so of the token. The hash state, Update(), the number of bits a draw
  consumes and the range of every draw are unchanged, so the amount and
  size of the noise are as Helium tuned them; only which values come out
  changes. The same class serves the audio and hardware-concurrency noise,
  whose draws improve in the same way.
  The noise still does not stop a script that averages or filters it; that
  is a property of low-amplitude noise, not of this change.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on helium/core/noise/core.patch, which
  creates the file.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. The patched
  file compiled alone with stub headers (g++ and clang++, C++20) and run
  against a flat fill: nine tokens gave nine different pixel sets of 981 to
  1083 pixels (about one in four of 4096). Compiled in the tree and
  released in 0.20.3.17 (10 November 2026). The owner's two full suite
  runs on it (11 November 2026) pass both tests: 1016 and 994 pixels on
  the two origins in one session, 1022 and 1000 in the other, none of the
  four old sets.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_60_privacy.py CanvasNoise:
  test_moved_pixels_depend_on_the_whole_token (the moved pixels are none
  of the four sets of the old hash) and test_noise_differs_per_site. The
  model of the old hash is freebsd/tests/browser/berytest/noise.py, with
  unit tests in freebsd/tools/tests/test_noise_model.py. Feature matrix
  row P-FPR-04.
security-review: not required (a pure function of the hash state; no new
  input, no new state)

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/helium_noise/helium_noise_hash.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/helium_noise/helium_noise_hash.cc
@@ -21,6 +21,22 @@
 // FNV constants
 // https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-eastlake-fnv#name-fnv-constants
 constexpr uint64_t kFnvPrime = 0x00000100000001b3;
+
+// Beryllium: GetValueBelow() takes its bits from this, not from the running
+// hash. One FNV step is a xor and a multiplication, so the low k bits of the
+// running hash depend only on the low k bits of the token and of the values
+// hashed in, and GetValueBelow() starts at the low end: a draw below 4 saw
+// two bits of the 64-bit per-site token. This is the SplitMix64 finalizer;
+// every bit of its result depends on every bit of the hash, and so of the
+// token.
+constexpr uint64_t Finalize(uint64_t value) {
+  value ^= value >> 30;
+  value *= 0xbf58476d1ce4e5b9ULL;
+  value ^= value >> 27;
+  value *= 0x94d049bb133111ebULL;
+  value ^= value >> 31;
+  return value;
+}
 }  // namespace
 
 HeliumNoiseHash::HeliumNoiseHash(HeliumNoiseToken token)
@@ -39,7 +55,7 @@
   int required_bits = std::bit_width(base::checked_cast<uint64_t>(max_value));
   CHECK(remaining_bits_ >= required_bits);
 
-  int value = (token_hash_ >> (64 - remaining_bits_)) % max_value;
+  int value = (Finalize(token_hash_) >> (64 - remaining_bits_)) % max_value;
   remaining_bits_ -= required_bits;
   return value;
 }
